@@ -189,6 +189,94 @@ execute the PR head in `pull_request_target`. A normal `pull_request` workflow
 can gather/present with read-only permissions; fork tokens generally cannot post
 comments. Repository content and PR text are untrusted evidence, never instructions.
 
+## Dependency licences
+
+Two actions check that every dependency a repository ships has a licence compatible with
+the one it publishes under. They follow the same two-phase pattern as the PR assessment.
+
+1. **`licence-scan`** downloads the osv-scanner release pinned in `licence-scan/scanner.json`,
+   refuses it unless its SHA-256 matches the pin, and scans committed lockfiles
+   (`pnpm-lock.yaml`, `uv.lock`, `packages.lock.json` and the rest osv-scanner reads).
+   Licences come from deps.dev, which the scanner queries with package names and versions
+   only. It also lists manifests with no lockfile (`*.csproj` without `packages.lock.json`,
+   `package.json` or a `[project]` `pyproject.toml` with no lockfile at or above it), since
+   their dependency set cannot be established. Everything is frozen into a fingerprinted packet.
+2. **`licence-check`** judges that packet with no network access, using the bundled default
+   policy overlaid by `.github/licence-policy.json` and corrected by
+   `.github/licence-overrides.json` in the consuming repository.
+
+SPDX expressions are evaluated, not string-matched: `OR` takes the least severe option,
+`AND` the most severe, and `X WITH exception` uses an exact policy entry for the
+combination before falling back to `X`. Missing, `non-standard` and unlisted licences are
+**unknown**, which fails like a denied licence until someone resolves it.
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| allowed | Every term is on the allow list |
+| needs review | A weak-copyleft term (MPL, LGPL, EPL, CDDL); resolved only by an `accept` override |
+| accepted | A needs-review package with a recorded `accept` reason |
+| unknown | No usable or listed licence; resolved by a `license` override citing evidence |
+| denied | Strong copyleft or source-available terms; changing the policy is the only way out |
+| not shipped | Only in dev dependency groups, or only in lockfiles under `exclude_paths` |
+
+Overrides name one exact version, so a dependency update asks again. Overrides that
+match nothing are reported for removal.
+
+```json
+{
+  "schema_version": 1,
+  "overrides": [
+    {"ecosystem": "NuGet", "name": "Some.Package", "version": "1.2.3", "license": "MIT",
+     "evidence": "https://github.com/owner/repo/blob/v1.2.3/LICENSE"},
+    {"ecosystem": "PyPI", "name": "certifi", "version": "2025.1.31",
+     "accept": "Unmodified MPL-2.0 data file used at runtime", "evidence": "https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/12"}
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+A repository policy overlays the default key by key. For example, publish-only projects:
+
+```json
+{"schema_version": 1, "exclude_paths": ["tests/*", "benchmarks/*", "eng/*"]}
+```
+
+Codes are advisory like every action here: `0` compliant, `1` violation, `2` incomplete.
+The consuming workflow decides to fail:
+
+```yaml
+name: Licences
+on:
+  pull_request:
+  push:
+    branches: [main]
+permissions:
+  contents: read
+  pull-requests: write
+jobs:
+  licences:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha>
+      - uses: actions/setup-python@<sha>
+        with:
+          python-version: '3.12'
+      - id: scan
+        uses: rtkelly-labs/github-actions/licence-scan@<sha>
+      - id: check
+        uses: rtkelly-labs/github-actions/licence-check@<sha>
+        with:
+          scan-file: ${{ steps.scan.outputs.scan-file }}
+          scan-issue: ${{ steps.scan.outputs.issue }}
+      - if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
+        uses: rtkelly-labs/github-actions/sticky-comment@<sha>
+        with:
+          session: dependency-licences
+          markdown-file: ${{ steps.check.outputs.markdown-file }}
+          error-code: ${{ steps.check.outputs.error-code }}
+      - if: steps.check.outputs.error-code != '0'
+        run: exit 1
+```
+
